.TH VRUNNERCTRL 1 "2025" "vrunner" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
vrunnerctrl \- CLI controller for vrunner instances (subcommands of vrunner)

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vrunner
\fBlist\fR

.B vrunner
\fBstop\fR \fIPID\fR

.B vrunner
\fBcert generate\fR \fINAME\fR

.B vrunner
\fBcert list\fR

.B vrunner
\fBcert show\fR \fINAME\fR

.B vrunner
\fBcert remove\fR \fINAME\fR

.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBvrunnerctrl\fR is not a separate binary. It documents the CLI controller subcommands built into the \fBvrunner\fR(1) binary. These subcommands connect to running vrunner instances over HTTP to inspect, manage, and shut them down remotely. They are the command-line equivalent of the web admin interface.

When you run \fBvrunner list\fR, \fBvrunner stop\fR, or \fBvrunner cert\fR, vrunner acts as a controller \[em] it does not start a server. Instead, it queries or sends commands to an already-running instance.

For the full server binary documentation, see \fBvrunner\fR(1).

.SH SUBCOMMANDS

.SS list

List all running vrunner instances on the system. Each vrunner instance registers itself in a shared registry when it starts, allowing discovery from the CLI.

.RS
.PP
.B Usage:
.RS
vrunner list
.RE
.RE

.PP
Output columns:
.RS
.TP
.B PID
Process ID of the vrunner instance.
.TP
.B PORT
TCP port the instance is listening on.
.TP
.B BIND
Bind address (e.g., \fB127.0.0.1\fR or \fB0.0.0.0\fR).
.TP
.B DAEMON
Whether the instance is running as a background daemon (\fByes\fR/\fBno\fR).
.TP
.B DISPLAY
Whether local VTTY display is enabled (\fByes\fR/\fBno\fR).
.TP
.B COMMAND
The initial command the instance was started with, or \fB(idle)\fR if no command was given.
.RE

.PP
Example output:
.RS
.PP
.nf
PID        PORT     BIND                 DAEMON     DISPLAY    COMMAND
12345      8080     127.0.0.1            yes        no         (idle)
12346      9090     127.0.0.1            no         yes        vim
.RE

.SS stop

Gracefully shut down a running vrunner instance by its process ID. This sends an HTTP \fBPOST /api/shutdown\fR request to the instance, allowing it to clean up resources, kill managed commands, and exit cleanly.

.RS
.PP
.B Usage:
.RS
vrunner stop \fIPID\fR
.RE
.RE

.PP
If the instance is unresponsive (e.g., frozen or network issue), the command may time out. In that case, use \fBkill(1)\fR or \fBkill -9\fR as a fallback.

.PP
Example:
.RS
.PP
.nf
\fB$ vrunner list\fR
PID        PORT     BIND        DAEMON     COMMAND
12345      8080     127.0.0.1   yes        (idle)
12346      9090     127.0.0.1   yes        htop

\fB$ vrunner stop 12346\fR
Sent shutdown request to PID 12346 (port 9090)
.RE

.SS cert generate

Generate a new self-signed X.509 certificate and add it to the certificate pool. The certificate is stored in \fB~/.config/vrunner/certs/<name>/\fR with a derived bearer token (SHA-256 hash of the certificate PEM, hex-encoded as 64 characters).

.RS
.PP
.B Usage:
.RS
vrunner cert generate \fINAME\fR
.RE
.RE

.PP
Arguments:
.RS
.TP
.B NAME
A unique identifier for the certificate (e.g., \fBmy-app\fR, \fBteam-alpha\fR, \fBci-pipeline\fR). Used to reference the certificate when binding it to commands.
.RE

.PP
Output:
.RS
.PP
.nf
Certificate 'my-app' generated successfully.
  Certificate: /home/user/.config/vrunner/certs/my-app/cert.pem
  Key:        /home/user/.config/vrunner/certs/my-app/key.pem
  Token:      a3f8c1e2b7d94001... (first 16 of 64 chars)
.RE

.SS cert list

List all certificates configured in the certificate pool. Reads from the configuration file (global or local \fBvrunner.yaml\fR/\fBvrunner.toml\fR) and displays each certificate's name, file path, and the first 16 characters of its derived bearer token.

.RS
.PP
.B Usage:
.RS
vrunner cert list
.RE
.RE

.PP
Output:
.RS
.PP
.nf
NAME                     CERT FILE                                         TOKEN (prefix)
my-app                   /home/user/.config/vrunner/certs/my-app/cert.pem   a3f8c1e2b7d94001
ci-pipeline              /home/user/.config/vrunner/certs/ci-pipeline/cert.pem 7c1e9d4a8f2...
.RE

.SS cert show

Display full details of a specific certificate, including the complete 64-character bearer token. This token is used in the \fBAuthorization: Bearer\fR header when making API requests to certificate-bound commands.

.RS
.PP
.B Usage:
.RS
vrunner cert show \fINAME\fR
.RE
.RE

.PP
Output:
.RS
.PP
.nf
Certificate: my-app
  Certificate: /home/user/.config/vrunner/certs/my-app/cert.pem
  Key:        /home/user/.config/vrunner/certs/my-app/key.pem
  Token:      a3f8c1e2b7d94001... (full SHA-256 hex)
  Token (16): a3f8c1e2b7d94001...
.RE

.SS cert remove

Remove a certificate from the pool registry. This removes the certificate from vrunner's internal registry so it can no longer be used to bind commands. The PEM files on disk are \fBnot\fR deleted.

.RS
.PP
.B Usage:
.RS
vrunner cert remove \fINAME\fR
.RE
.RE

.SH HTTP API CONTROLLER

The CLI subcommands are wrappers around the vrunner HTTP API. You can also control vrunner instances directly with \fBcurl\fR(1) or any HTTP client. This is useful for scripting, automation, and integration with other tools.

.SS Authentication

When the server has auth enabled (via \fB\-\-auth\fR, \fB\-\-remote\fR, or configuration), include a bearer token:

.RS
.PP
.nf
export TOKEN=$(cat ~/.config/vrunner/token)
curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" http://localhost:8080/api/commands
.fi
.RE

.SS Listing commands

.RS
.PP
.nf
\fB# Plain HTTP (localhost, no auth)\fR
curl http://localhost:8080/api/commands

\fB# HTTPS with auth (remote)\fR
curl --cacert ~/.config/vrunner/cert.pem \e
     -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \e
     https://server:8080/api/commands

\fB# Pretty-print JSON\fR
curl -s http://localhost:8080/api/commands | python -m json.tool
.fi
.RE

.SS Spawning a command

.RS
.PP
.nf
\fB# Start htop\fR
curl -s -X POST http://localhost:8080/api/commands \e
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \e
  -d '{"cmd": "htop", "args": []}'

\fB# Start with arguments\fR
curl -s -X POST http://localhost:8080/api/commands \e
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \e
  -d '{"cmd": "python", "args": ["-m", "http.server", "8000"]}'

\fB# Start bound to a certificate\fR
curl -s -X POST http://localhost:8080/api/commands \e
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \e
  -d '{"cmd": "node", "args": ["server.js"], "certificate": "my-app"}'

\fB# Save the command ID for later use\fR
ID=$(curl -s -X POST http://localhost:8080/api/commands \e
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \e
  -d '{"cmd": "htop", "args": []}' | python -c "import sys,json; print(json.load(sys.stdin)['data']['id'])")
.fi
.RE

.SS Sending keystrokes

.RS
.PP
.nf
\fB# Quit htop\fR
curl -s -X POST "http://localhost:8080/api/commands/$ID/keys" \e
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \e
  -d '{"keys": "q"}'

\fB# Type text and press Enter\fR
curl -s -X POST "http://localhost:8080/api/commands/$ID/keys" \e
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \e
  -d '{"keys": "ls -la\\r"}'

\fB# Send Ctrl+C\fR
curl -s -X POST "http://localhost:8080/api/commands/$ID/keys" \e
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \e
  -d '{"keys": "\\x03"}'

\fB# Quit vim: Escape, then :q! Enter\fR
curl -s -X POST "http://localhost:8080/api/commands/$ID/keys" \e
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \e
  -d '{"keys": "\\x1b:q!\\r"}'
.fi
.RE

Common escape sequences for the \fBkeys\fR field:
.RS
.TP
.B \\x03
Ctrl+C (SIGINT)
.TP
.B \\x04
Ctrl+D (EOF)
.TP
.B \\x1b
Escape
.TP
.B \\r
Enter/Return
.TP
.B \\t
Tab
.TP
.B \\x7f
Backspace
.TP
.B \\x1b[A
Up arrow
.TP
.B \\x1b[B
Down arrow
.TP
.B \\x1b[C
Right arrow
.TP
.B \\x1b[D
Left arrow
.RE

.SS Viewing terminal output

.RS
.PP
.nf
\fB# Raw ANSI output\fR
curl http://localhost:8080/api/commands/$ID/vtty

\fB# HTML-rendered output (with cursor position and dimensions)\fR
curl http://localhost:8080/api/commands/$ID/vtty/html

\fB# Partial content (paginated)\fR
curl "http://localhost:8080/api/commands/$ID/vtty/partial?offset=0&limit=50"

\fB# Extract just the HTML for embedding\fR
curl -s http://localhost:8080/api/commands/$ID/vtty/html | \e
  python -c "import sys,json; print(json.load(sys.stdin)['data']['html'])"
.fi
.RE

.SS Resizing the terminal

.RS
.PP
.nf
curl -s -X POST "http://localhost:8080/api/commands/$ID/resize" \e
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \e
  -d '{"rows": 40, "cols": 120}'
.fi
.RE

Valid ranges: rows 1-200, cols 1-500.

.SS Killing a command

.RS
.PP
.nf
\fB# Graceful kill (SIGTERM)\fR
curl -s -X POST "http://localhost:8080/api/commands/$ID/kill" \e
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \e
  -d '{}'

\fB# Force kill (SIGKILL)\fR
curl -s -X POST "http://localhost:8080/api/commands/$ID/kill" \e
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \e
  -d '{"signal": "SIGKILL"}'
.fi
.RE

.SS Searching command logs

.RS
.PP
.nf
\fB# Get recent logs\fR
curl "http://localhost:8080/api/log?limit=50"

\fB# Search for spawn events\fR
curl "http://localhost:8080/api/log?search=spawn&limit=100"

\fB# Paginated search\fR
curl "http://localhost:8080/api/log?search=kill&offset=0&limit=50"
curl "http://localhost:8080/api/log?search=kill&offset=50&limit=50"
.fi
.RE

.SS Getting server info

.RS
.PP
.nf
curl http://localhost:8080/api/info
.fi
.RE

Returns command count, certificate count, certificate names, and whether auth is enabled.

.SS Listing certificates

.RS
.PP
.nf
curl http://localhost:8080/api/certificates
.fi
.RE

.SS Shutting down the server

.RS
.PP
.nf
curl -s -X POST http://localhost:8080/api/shutdown
.fi
.RE

.SS WebSocket endpoints

vrunner also supports WebSocket connections for real-time streaming:

.RS
.TP
.B GET /api/commands/:id/ws
Real-time VTTY output streaming. After connecting, the server sends JSON messages with type \fBvtty_update\fR whenever the terminal content changes. Clients can send \fBkeys\fR and \fBresize\fR messages.

.TP
.B GET /api/ws/logs
Real-time log entry streaming. The server pushes each new log entry as a JSON message with type \fBlog_entry\fR.
.RE

WebSocket connection example with \fBwebsocat\fR(1):
.RS
.PP
.nf
\fB# Stream VTTY output in real time\fR
websocat "ws://localhost:8080/api/commands/$ID/ws"

\fB# Stream logs in real time\fR
websocat "ws://localhost:8080/api/ws/logs"
.fi
.RE

.SH SCRIPTING EXAMPLES

.SS Automating a build job

.RS
.PP
.nf
#!/bin/bash
HOST="http://localhost:8080"

# Start a build
ID=$(curl -s -X POST "$HOST/api/commands" \e
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \e
  -d '{"cmd": "./build.sh", "args": ["--release"]}' \e
  | python -c "import sys,json; print(json.load(sys.stdin)['data']['id'])")

echo "Build started: $ID"

# Wait for completion (poll every 5 seconds)
while true; do
  STATUS=$(curl -s "$HOST/api/commands" \e
    | python -c "
import sys, json
cmds = json.load(sys.stdin)['data']
for c in cmds:
    if c['id'] == '$ID':
        print(c['status'])
        break
" 2>/dev/null)
  [ -z "$STATUS" ] && echo "Build finished" && break
  sleep 5
done

# Retrieve the full build log
curl -s "$HOST/api/commands/$ID/vtty"
.fi
.RE

.SS Managing multiple instances

.RS
.PP
.nf
#!/bin/bash
# Stop all idle instances
vrunner list | while read pid port bind daemon display cmd; do
  # Skip header
  [ "$pid" = "PID" ] && continue
  if [ "$cmd" = "(idle)" ]; then
    echo "Stopping idle instance $pid on port $port..."
    vrunner stop "$pid"
  fi
done
.fi
.RE

.SS Certificate-based workflow

.RS
.PP
.nf
#!/bin/bash
HOST="http://localhost:8080"

# Generate a certificate for this deployment
vrunner cert generate "deploy-$(date +%Y%m%d)"

# Get the token
TOKEN=$(vrunner cert show "deploy-$(date +%Y%m%d)" \e
  | grep "Token (16)" | awk '{print $3}')...

# Start a deployment bound to this cert
ID=$(curl -s -X POST "$HOST/api/commands" \e
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \e
  -d "{\"cmd\": \"./deploy.sh\", \"args\": [], \"certificate\": \"deploy-$(date +%Y%m%d)\"}" \e
  | python -c "import sys,json; print(json.load(sys.stdin)['data']['id'])")

# Monitor with cert-specific token
curl -s -H "Authorization: Bearer $FULL_TOKEN" \e
  "$HOST/api/commands/$ID/vtty/html"
.fi
.RE

.SH API RESPONSE FORMAT

All API responses use a standard JSON envelope:

.RS
.PP
.nf
{
  "status": "ok",
  "data": { ... },
  "error": null
}
.fi
.RE

Error responses:
.RS
.PP
.nf
{
  "status": "error",
  "data": null,
  "error": "Description of what went wrong"
}
.fi
.RE

The HTTP status code is always 200 (the envelope distinguishes success from failure). Check the \fBstatus\fR field to determine the outcome.

.SH COMPLETE API REFERENCE

.TP
.B GET /api/commands
List all running commands.

.TP
.B POST /api/commands
Start a new command. Body: \fB{"cmd": "...", "args": [...], "certificate": "..."|null}\fR

.TP
.B POST /api/commands/:id/keys
Send keystrokes. Body: \fB{"keys": "..."}\fR

.TP
.B POST /api/commands/:id/kill
Kill a command. Body: \fB{"signal": "..."}\fR (optional)

.TP
.B GET /api/commands/:id/vtty
Get full VTTY contents as raw ANSI text.

.TP
.B GET /api/commands/:id/vtty/html
Get VTTY contents as rendered HTML with cursor and dimensions.

.TP
.B GET /api/commands/:id/vtty/partial?offset=N&limit=N
Get paginated VTTY content.

.TP
.B POST /api/commands/:id/resize
Resize virtual terminal. Body: \fB{"rows": N, "cols": N}\fR

.TP
.B GET /api/commands/:id/handles
List output handles for a command.

.TP
.B POST /api/commands/:id/handles
Add an output handle to a command.

.TP
.B GET /api/commands/:id/ws
WebSocket endpoint for real-time VTTY streaming.

.TP
.B GET /api/certificates
List all certificates in the pool.

.TP
.B GET /api/info
Get instance information (counts, auth status).

.TP
.B GET /api/log?search=TERM&offset=N&limit=N
Get command log entries with optional search and pagination.

.TP
.B GET /api/ws/logs
WebSocket endpoint for real-time log streaming.

.TP
.B POST /api/shutdown
Gracefully shut down the vrunner instance.

.SH FILES

.TP
.B ~/.config/vrunner/token
Default bearer token for API authentication.

.TP
.B ~/.config/vrunner/cert.pem
Auto-generated TLS server certificate.

.TP
.B ~/.config/vrunner/certs/
Directory for certificate pool entries.

.SH ENVIRONMENT

.TP
.B VRUNNER_TOKEN
Can be used to store the bearer token for scripts (read by convention; vrunner itself uses \fB\-\-token\-file\fR).

.TP
.B VRUNNER_CACERT
Can be used to store the CA certificate path for scripts (read by convention).

.SH EXIT STATUS

.TP
.B 0
Successful operation.

.TP
.B 1
Error (instance not found, connection refused, invalid arguments, etc.).

